Exports the native bodies of `[]`, `[]=` and the class-level `[]` as explicit-argument
functions, so bc2cpp-generated index helpers can call mruby's own code instead of
dispatching by name (docs/adr/0365).

The static natives read their arguments with mrb_get_args/mrb_get_arg1, which look at
the CALLING frame; a shared C helper has a different frame. Each body is therefore split
in two: a non-static `<name>_impl` that takes its arguments as parameters and holds the
whole body, and the original method, which still reads its arguments the way it did and
calls the impl. Interpreted behaviour does not change: an `i` of mrb_get_args is
mrb_as_int (Array's aget_index1 spells that out for the one-argument forms), and every
other statement moved verbatim.

  array.c        mrb_ary_aget1_impl    Array#[] with one argument (index or Range)
                 mrb_ary_aset2_impl    Array#[]= with an index and a value
                 mrb_ary_s_create_impl Array.[]
  string.c       mrb_str_aset          String#[]= (no frame reads; was static)
  struct.c       mrb_struct_aref_impl, mrb_struct_aset_impl
  hash_ext.c     mrb_hash_s_create_impl  Hash.[]
  set.c          mrb_set_s_create_impl   Set.[]
  proc.c, vm.c   mrb_proc_aref_impl      Proc#[]: mrb_funcall_with_block with the method
                 (call_proc) already found; vm.c's funcall_with_block_m takes an optional
                 fixed method and owner, and mrb_funcall_with_block passes none.

Hash#[], Hash#[]=, String#[], Struct classes' `.[]` need nothing here: mrb_hash_get,
mrb_hash_set, mrb_str_aref and mrb_obj_new are public and read no frame. Each declaration
sits in MRB_BEGIN_DECL so the symbols keep C linkage when a build compiles the core as C++.

